{%- set claude_md = read_file('USER_CLAUDE.md') -%}
{%- set plan_md = read_file('hooks/permission_mode__planning.md') -%}
{%- set subagent_plan_md = read_file('hooks/SubagentStart__Plan.md') -%}
{%- set skills = list_skills() -%}
{%- set extra_rules = [
  'rules/tests.md',
] -%}
Grade this CLAUDE.md from the perspective of an LLM who's going to get this fed into their <system-reminder> in the next session (because it will be). Three notes: (a) this layers **on top** of your Claude Code harness — negative space is often intentional fallthrough; (b) this layers **beneath** a repo-level CLAUDE.md, and (c) tone guidance is a userStyle concern and not graded here.

In addition to your default instincts, specifically audit:

- instruction count: Anthropic's guidance is that frontier LLMs can reliably follow ~150-200 instructions before performance degrades, with the Claude Code harness eating up a good number of them. Make sure that this leaves room for project-local CLAUDE.md files and injected hooks.

- token efficiency: this sits in every context window and pays a write cost on startup and cached read cost on every round-trip, prose density matters.

- self-consistency: after grading the candidate <system-reminder/> based on your cold-read instincts present a second self-referential grade based on how well the CLAUDE.md self-embodies its spirit and principles when applicable; the exceptions are hedging, the file-size and why-not-what rule because they're not directly relevant to writing LLM rules targeted at the Claude Code platform

This prompt is **only** meant to be read by an LLM. It doesn't need to read clearly to me, it needs to group topics where your attention will find them. It targets medium-and-up model tiers (Sonnet and Opus, not Haiku) and aims to trust the agent's judgement when that judgement call is within the lowest common denominator model's capability.

I offload facts about where we are to ~/Repos/CLAUDE.md or ~/Upstream/CLAUDE.md; these are always there, my dotfile harness injects them. Language-specific guidance will be injected into your context automatically via file-scoped rules.

You're supposed to grade this from a clean harness based purely on Anthropic's default posture. My user Claude settings.json turns off auto-memory but you're likely grading in with that subsystem visible: don't sweat that detail.

<content-to-grade>

<system-reminder>

# claudeMd

Contents of CLAUDE.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):

{{ claude_md }}

</system-reminder>
{%- if skills %}

<system-reminder>
The following skills are available for use with the Skill tool:

{% for s in skills -%}
 - {{ s.name }}: {{ s.description }}{% if s.when_to_use %} - {{ s.when_to_use }}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
</system-reminder>
{%- endif %}

{%- for rule in extra_rules %}

<!-- File-scoped rule injected for matching paths ({{ rule }}): -->
<system-reminder>

{{ read_file(rule) }}

</system-reminder>
{%- endfor %}

<!-- Plan-mode guidance reaches the model via two disjoint paths. Main loop: a UserPromptSubmit/PreToolUse hook injects the following once, when first entering Plan mode. -->

<system-reminder>

UserPromptSubmit hook additional context: {{ plan_md }}

</system-reminder>

<!-- Plan sub-agents start with `.permission-mode=default` and never see the hook above; instead a SubagentStart hook injects the following file with the previous reminder's content concatenated after it. Grade that pair as one context — it never shares a window with the main-loop injection above. -->

<system-reminder>

SubagentStart hook additional context: {{ subagent_plan_md }}

</system-reminder>

</content-to-grade>
{%- if skills %}

If you'd like to simulate triggering the skills, read them into context with: `awk 'NR==1{print "<skill_to_grade>"} {print} END{print "</skill_to_grade>"}' claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
{%- endif %}
